 "meta": {
  "when": "Measured 4 Oct 2026, one program at a time, before then after, back to back.",
  "edits": "<ul>\n<li><b>Compiler:</b> <code>@inline</code> and <code>@dont(inline)</code> on a function. <code>@inline</code> inlines every call that is safe to inline, even with <code>function-inlining: off</code> and past the preset's size and growth limits; the safety rules (no <code>@async</code>, no recursion cycle, scalar parameters, ...) still apply. The optimizer reports what it did (<code>[note] file:line - @inline: f inlined at N call sites</code>) and warns about calls it could not inline.</li>\n<li><b>Benchmark programs:</b> <code>@inline</code> on the six helper functions of <code>nbody_natural_f64</code> (<code>length, speedSquared, kinetic, potential, attract, drift</code>) and on <code>advance</code> in <code>nbody_arr</code> and <code>nbody_arr_f64</code>. Nothing else: in every other optimised program the aggressive preset already inlines every call that matters (checked by listing the calls left in the optimised bytecode), and trying <code>@inline</code> where calls did remain (merkletrees) or <code>@unroll</code> (hand-unrolled by 4: 3%) gave nothing measurable.</li>\n<li><b>Stdlib:</b> <code>@inline</code> on <code>DynamicArray.get / set / setPtr</code> and <code>String.charAt / setCharAt</code>.</li></ul>",
  "method": "<p>Quick benchmark, Caspien only, optimised builds (every optimisation on, <code>loop-unrolling: aggressive</code>, <code>function-inlining: aggressive</code>), best of 3 runs. For each program the old tree (commit <code>4fde78a</code>: old programs, old stdlib, old compiler) was measured first and the new tree straight after, so slow drift of the machine hits both. Outputs of every row match the C reference.</p>",
  "caveats": "<ul>\n<li>Machine noise is 10&ndash;30% between measurements minutes apart (an earlier before/after taken 25 minutes apart showed sieve 39% slower with byte-identical assembly). Back-to-back pairing reduces it but does not remove it; rows inside the grey band are unchanged.</li>\n<li>Only the three marked n-body programs were edited. For the unedited ones I compared the generated assembly of the old and new compiler on sieve (safe, naive) and graph (safe): identical, so their movement is machine noise; I did not compare the rest.</li>\n<li>The stdlib chart uses best of 3 per preset, measured one after the other, not the full harness.</li>\n<li><code>@unroll</code> was not used in any program: nearly every hot loop has a variable bound, which the unroller cannot unroll, and unrolling a loop with literal bounds was worth about 2&ndash;3% (loop-unrolling aggressive vs off).</li>\n<li>Linux/x86-64 only; Windows and Wine were not measured.</li></ul>"
 }
